What to build first (sequence that works)

Phase 1: “Backend skeleton” (1–2 days)
Create API project
Health check endpoint /health
Connect DB
Migrations enabled

Phase 2: Auth + user profile (1–2 days)
Login handled by Entra External ID (or even temporary email/password with bcrypt locally)
Backend middleware validates token
Create user record on first login

Phase 3: Problems (2–4 days)
DB tables: problems, test_cases
Admin endpoints to add problems
Public endpoints to list/search problems

Phase 4: Submissions (2–4 days)

Endpoint: POST /problems/:id/submit
Save submission row with status queued
Call Judge service
Update submission status (Accepted, Wrong Answer, TLE, Runtime Error)

Phase 5: Leaderboard / history (later)

User submission history
Best-time per problem, etc.
Minimal database schema (MVP)
users
id (uuid)
external_id (sub/oid from auth)
email, created_at
problems
id, title, difficulty, statement, constraints, created_at
test_cases
id, problem_id, input, expected_output, is_hidden
submissions
id, user_id, problem_id, language, code, status, stdout, stderr, created_at
MVP judging flow (how it works)

User submits code
Backend fetches test cases for that problem
Backend sends code + test input to Judge0 (or judge service)
Compare output vs expected
Store result + return response



Where you should start today (no overthinking)

Step 1: Choose your backend stack
Pick one:
FastAPI (Python) if you want fastest API dev
Node + Express/NestJS if you want JS everywhere
.NET if you want enterprise Azure-style

Step 2: Run local DB + judge using Docker
Postgres container
Judge0 container (or alternative)

Step 3: Build just 3 endpoints

GET /problems
GET /problems/:id
POST /problems/:id/submit

